From 93ad43f15262b168e23463d9de7ed122a62d7061 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 04:24:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] layout: lay out ruby annotations above their bases
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

<ruby> rendered its annotations inline ("漢かん字じ") because Servo
dropped display: ruby and display: ruby-text, where Chrome stacks each
reading above its base, which Japanese news and education pages rely on.

With Stylo parsing both values, the block container builder turns a ruby
into an inline box (or, when blockified, a block) holding one atomic
inline per ruby column. ruby_items pairs each ruby-text child with the
content before it, skips display: none children such as <rp>, and keeps
white space between columns as a space. A column is a block container,
styled by the new ::-servo-ruby-column pseudo-element, with the annotation
as a block above the base, so it is as wide as the wider of the two,
centers both, and takes its baseline from the base. Its anonymous base
block nests under the column pseudo-element so that it inherits the
column's text-align and line-height. Inline layout leaves the annotation
out of the column's line height contribution, so it overflows upwards
into the leading like in Chrome instead of growing the line. The UA sheet
sizes annotations like Chrome's.

Ruby text containers, nested rubies and Chrome's extra line spacing for
annotations that do not fit above a line are not handled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
